  "readingHtml": "<h1>The Future of Space Tourism</h1><div class=\"subtitle\">Will you travel to space?</div><p><span class=\"para-label\">A.</span>In the future, traveling to space will not just be for astronauts. Companies are working hard to <b>establish</b> a new industry: space tourism. Next year, they <b>are going to launch</b> the first commercial flights. By 2030, ordinary people <b>will be orbiting</b> the Earth in luxury space hotels.</p><p><span class=\"para-label\">B.</span>However, this type of travel <b>requires</b> strict rules. You <b>have to</b> be in good health. You <b>must not</b> have heart problems. Children under 18 <b>are not allowed to</b> fly alone. Space is full of <b>hazards</b>, such as space debris. While you <b>are flying</b>, you <b>must</b> wear a special suit. Passengers <b>should</b> also train for a few days before the flight.</p><p><span class=\"para-label\">C.</span>Humans <b>are not adapted</b> to zero gravity. In space, your body <b>will undergo</b> many changes: your muscles get weaker, and many people feel sick on the first day. You might also feel <b>isolated</b> or homesick. Doctors say that most healthy passengers <b>will manage</b> to <b>survive</b> the journey without serious problems. And most travellers say the journey is worth it, because the view of Earth is unforgettable. Would you <b>be willing to</b> pay millions of dollars for a ticket? Only time will tell.</p><p><span class=\"para-label\">D.</span>The first tourist flight <b>is going to leave</b> from a spaceport in the desert next March. The journey <b>will last</b> three days, and an astronaut <b>will travel</b> with every group. Before the launch, every passenger <b>has to</b> train for three days at the spaceport. A ticket costs about 450,000 dollars, so at first only a few people <b>can</b> afford one. Engineers believe that prices <b>will fall</b> by 2035, and that one day a trip to space <b>will feel</b> as normal as a long flight.</p>",
